From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Grenz <info@ryangrenz.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 23:26:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 11/14] drm/bridge: dw-mipi-dsi: request the HS clock only in
 video mode

dw_mipi_dsi_set_mode() requests the continuous high-speed clock in
command mode as well as in video mode. A panel that sets
prepare_prev_first is reset and sent its init sequence in that command
mode window, after the host has already put the clock lane into HS. The
JD9365 panel on an inMusic Prime 4+ never comes out of that: the
backlight is on, every command is accepted without error, and the
panel stays dark.

u-boot's port of this driver, which initialises the same panel
correctly before every boot, leaves the clock lane in low-power in
command mode and requests HS only for video. Do the same here, so the
panel receives its low-power init sequence with the clock lane idle and
sees the clock lane enter HS when video starts.

Panels that send their init sequence from enable(), in video mode, are
unaffected, as are panels without prepare_prev_first, whose prepare()
runs before the host enters command mode.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [not yet tested beyond the Prime 4+; mainline still requests HS in command mode]
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c | 9 ++++++---
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c
index 8fc2e282ff11..a77ade14141f 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/synopsys/dw-mipi-dsi.c
@@ -639,9 +639,12 @@ static void dw_mipi_dsi_set_mode(struct dw_mipi_dsi *dsi,
 		dsi_write(dsi, DSI_MODE_CFG, ENABLE_CMD_MODE);
 	}
 
-	val = PHY_TXREQUESTCLKHS;
-	if (dsi->mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_NON_CONTINUOUS)
-		val |= AUTO_CLKLANE_CTRL;
+	val = 0;
+	if (mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO) {
+		val = PHY_TXREQUESTCLKHS;
+		if (dsi->mode_flags & MIPI_DSI_CLOCK_NON_CONTINUOUS)
+			val |= AUTO_CLKLANE_CTRL;
+	}
 	dsi_write(dsi, DSI_LPCLK_CTRL, val);
 
 	dsi_write(dsi, DSI_PWR_UP, POWERUP);
